<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adyghe Khabze</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> Khabze is the traditional unwritten code of conduct and customary law of the Circassians. It governs hospitality, honour, respect for elders, the duties between social ranks, relations between men and women, and behaviour in war and in assemblies. Passed down orally through generations, it was the basis of Circassian social order before the Russian conquest and remains an important part of Circassian identity in the homeland and the diaspora.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adyghe-khabze.jpg" alt="Adyghe Khabze" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liptink0 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signboard_in_Maykop_writing_the_proverb_%D0%9F%D1%81%D1%8D%D0%BC_%D1%8B%D0%BF%D1%8D_%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BF%D1%8D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Khabze literally means law and is formed from two roots, kha, society, and bze, language or code: the language of society. It has historically served as the unwritten constitution of Circassia. It once comprised three kinds of norms: everyday etiquette; ceremonial rules for weddings, funerals, childbirth and feasts; and legal rules on land use, class relations, military duties, inheritance and criminal liability, enforced by ad hoc courts and councils.</p><p>Its founding principle is that the individual is for the society: a person acts as a representative of family and lineage, and the community values them by their keeping of Khabze. Its goal is Adyghaghe, roughly Circassianity, which rests on five pillars: humanity, dignity, reason, courage and honour. A Circassian proverb says that if Adyghaghe disappears, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> will disappear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The rules were set and changed by the Khase, an assembly of representatives from all regions that decided by unanimous consensus on war, peace and alliances as well as everyday norms. Princes were not supposed to rule despotically or bypass Khabze. There were no prisons and, apart from rare cases, no flogging, which was seen as a violation of a free person's dignity; Khabze relied instead on public shame, shunning, material compensation, a blood price to halt blood revenge, and in extreme cases ostracism or execution.</p><p>A constant state of war militarised daily life. Worq Khabze, the code of the nobility and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> class, prized military prowess, emotional restraint and riding skill, and took its models from the Nart Sagas; it was a disgrace to attack an unarmed person, strike from behind or kill a sleeping enemy. Other rules guarded nature: three trees were to be planted for every one felled, and a third of the fruit was left for animals and birds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Khabze is not a religion. With pre-Christian and pre-Islamic roots, it absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a>, which accommodated it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-circassian-war">Russo-Circassian War</a> and the Circassian genocide of the 19th century it helped the diaspora survive, though in Turkey Kemalist policies of the 1920s and 1930s banned traditional dress and national instruments and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> to take Turkish surnames. It has since become a more flexible code of etiquette and identity, distinguished as Old and New Khabze, and some schools of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> teach it as a subject.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adyghe Khabze” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A strict unwritten code of conduct, <b>Adyghe Khabze</b>, regulated hospitality, honour, respect for elders, and relations between ranks and between men and women.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
